    1	//! Whether the machine, or this process's cgroup, is short of memory now.
    2	//!
    3	//! The scheduler weighs a compile by the memory it used before, but that
    4	//! cannot see what else is running: several agents' builds, an IDE, a
    5	//! browser. When memory runs short, a compile it admits anyway can push the
    6	//! machine into swap or the OOM killer. Under pressure, [`super::Scheduler`]
    7	//! therefore asks for the whole pool for a compile: it waits for the other
    8	//! compiles to finish, so they run one after another until pressure eases.
    9	//! Slots running tests hold still count against that need as before, so a
   10	//! test waiting on a compile cannot stall it.
   11	//!
   12	//! - macOS: the kernel's own verdict, `kern.memorystatus_vm_pressure_level`,
